# Turkey Energy Dashboard Project - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This project contains three interactive energy dashboards for Turkey with embedded data and professional chart export capabilities:

1. **dataset_a_primary_energy.html** - Primary Energy Production & Consumption (1972-2023)
2. **dataset_b_electricity.html** - Electricity Capacity & Generation by Sources  
3. **dataset_c_sectoral_consumption.html** - Sectoral Energy Consumption (1923-2023)

## File Modification Safety Protocol

### ⚠️ CRITICAL: Always Use Safe Modification Procedure
When making ANY changes to HTML files, ALWAYS follow this sequence:

```bash
# 1. Remove base64 logo data (prevents LLM crashes)
python remove_base64.py

# 2. Remove embedded JavaScript data (makes files safe to edit)
python remove_data.py

# 3. Make your changes to HTML files
# ... edit files ...

# 4. Restore base64 logo data
python restore_base64.py

# 5. Re-embed JavaScript data
python restore_data.py
```

### 🚨 Why This Process is Required:
- **Base64 data**: Large embedded logo data can crash LLM context
- **Embedded JS data**: 50-70KB of JavaScript data per file makes editing unsafe
- **WordPress compatibility**: Files need embedded data for WordPress deployment

## Utility Scripts

### Data Management
- `remove_base64.py` - Removes base64 logo data, replaces with placeholder
- `restore_base64.py` - Restores base64 logo from logo.jpg file
- `remove_data.py` - Removes embedded JavaScript data, restores external references
- `restore_data.py` - Embeds JavaScript data from data/ directory

### Data Conversion
- `csv_to_js.py` - Converts CSV files to JavaScript format
- `excel_to_js.py` - Converts Excel files to JavaScript format

## Data Sources
- `data/a/data_a_embedded.js` - Primary energy data (embeddedDataA)
- `data/b/data_b_embedded.js` - Electricity data (embeddedDataB)  
- `data/C/c_embedded_data.js` - Sectoral consumption data (embeddedRawData)

## Chart Quality Features

### Enhanced Legends
- **Line charts**: Truncated labels (25 chars), clickable toggles, line-style indicators
- **Bar charts**: Horizontal layout for >8 items, no redundant legends, full labels in tooltips
- **Pie charts**: Percentage displays, sorted by value, side legend for >8 items

### High-Quality Export
- **Resolution**: 1800×1200px at 3x pixel ratio (5400×3600 actual)
- **Typography**: Professional fonts with proper scaling
- **Logo**: Embedded high-res logo with drop shadow
- **Layout**: Proper margins and spacing for publication quality

### WordPress Compatibility
- **Font override**: `!important` declarations prevent serif font override
- **Color override**: `!important` declarations ensure white header text
- **Embedded data**: No external dependencies for WordPress upload

## Chart Improvements Applied

### Scaling & Visual Quality
- Increased canvas export resolution (1800×1200 @ 3x DPI)
- Better point visibility with hover effects
- Improved grid lines and typography
- Professional color palette with transparency

### Legend Structure
- **Line charts**: Line indicators, truncated labels, percentage space usage
- **Bar charts**: No legend (redundant), full labels in tooltips
- **Pie charts**: Percentage display, value-sorted, adaptive positioning

### Interactivity
- Enhanced tooltips with units and context
- Clickable legends for data series toggle
- Better hover effects and visual feedback
- Responsive layout for different screen sizes

## Development Guidelines

### Making Chart Modifications
1. Follow safety protocol above
2. Test on all three dashboard types
3. Verify export quality at high resolution
4. Check WordPress compatibility (fonts/colors)
5. Ensure mobile responsiveness

### Adding New Features
1. Implement in one file first
2. Test thoroughly
3. Apply to other files with consistent naming
4. Update this rules file with new procedures

### Color Scheme
- Primary: #006400 (dark green)
- Secondary: #228B22 (forest green)  
- Text: White on headers (!important for WordPress)
- Charts: Material Design color palette

### Font Requirements
- Primary: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif
- Use `!important` declarations for WordPress compatibility
- Maintain consistent sizing hierarchy

## File Structure
```
/
├── dataset_a_primary_energy.html (139KB with embedded data)
├── dataset_b_electricity.html (142KB with embedded data)
├── dataset_c_sectoral_consumption.html (135KB with embedded data)
├── logo.jpg (source logo file)
├── energy_dashboard_index.html (main index)
├── remove_base64.py (logo safety)
├── restore_base64.py (logo restoration)
├── remove_data.py (data safety)
├── restore_data.py (data embedding)
├── csv_to_js.py (data conversion)
├── excel_to_js.py (data conversion)
├── html_data_manager.py (advanced data management)
└── data/
    ├── a/data_a_embedded.js (primary energy)
    ├── b/data_b_embedded.js (electricity)
    └── C/c_embedded_data.js (sectoral consumption)
```

## Common Tasks

### Update Chart Styling
```bash
python remove_base64.py && python remove_data.py
# Edit HTML files with new styles
python restore_base64.py && python restore_data.py
```

### Change Data Source
```bash
# Update data files in data/ directory
python restore_data.py  # Re-embed updated data
```

### WordPress Deployment
- Files are ready for direct upload after embedding
- No external dependencies required
- Professional export quality maintained

## Best Practices
- Always test export functionality after chart modifications
- Verify legend readability with different data sets
- Check responsive behavior on mobile devices
- Maintain consistent styling across all three dashboards
- Document any new safety procedures in this file 